<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Gorbachev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2022">2022</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail Gorbachev (1931–2022) was the last leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, general secretary of the Communist Party from 1985 and president from 1990 to 1991. His policies of glasnost, or openness, and perestroika, or restructuring, loosened censorship and central control, while abroad he ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and allowed the fall of the communist regimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> in 1989. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev.jpg" alt="Mikhail Gorbachev" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Vyatkin / Владимир Вяткин; derivative work by Jbarta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_850809_General_Secretary_of_the_CPSU_CC_M._Gorbachev_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b> the Soviet bloc cut its aid, and in December 1990 the Soviet Union stopped aiding the PDRE altogether.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b>'s reforms, protests against phosphate mining in 1987 grew into a mass national movement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b>, environmental protests grew into the Popular Front of Latvia, founded in 1988, and the Baltic Way of 23 August 1989.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b>’s reforms the reform movement Sąjūdis was founded in 1988, and in February 1990 it won elections to the Supreme Council, which under Vytautas Landsbergis declared the restoration of independence on 11 March 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Supreme Soviet, created in 1938, had little real power until the reforms of <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time the Soviet Union under <b>Mikhail Gorbachev</b> cut back its aid, and the collapse of communism in Eastern Europe in 1989 removed the regime’s external model and support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
